    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d97">

                <pb facs="d97-01" n="1" xml:id="d97-01"/>

                <head>97. Notes of Telephone Conversation Between the President’s Assistant for
                    National Security Affairs (Kissinger) and Secretary of Defense Laird<anchor n="1" xml:id="d97-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d97-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1969-05-06T16:30:00-04:00">May 6, 1969, 4:30 p.m.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>[Omitted here is discussion unrelated to seabeds.]</p>
                <p>
                    L said he would do it, but they are concerned
                    about L’s Seabed thing. K said not to worry about it. K said
                    he called Dave on Seabed matter and if L could get
                    him some of his suggested changes he would appreciate it. L said they were sent over a couple of days ago. K said President then said he would talk to Gerry and
                    say his condition for going ahead with it would be to include L’s changes - that way he could buy more time on
                        SALT. L said
                    he understood. K said Pres will not make a ruling
                    like that until everyone has chance to express views.</p>
                <p>[Omitted here is discussion unrelated to seabeds.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d97-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d97-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Library of Congress, Manuscript Division, Kissinger Papers, Box 359, Telephone Conversations,
                    Chronological File, 1–14 May 1969. No classification marking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d97-f2" n="2" xml:id="d97-f2-content">
                <p>
                    Kissinger and Laird discussed the next steps in the “Seabed matter.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
